1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:06,100
Lee surrendered at Appomattox in April
1865. The Civil War's last surrender came

2
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:07,838
seven months later.

3
00:00:08,078 --> 00:00:12,178
Separate the surrenders on a
timeline and they keep coming.

4
00:00:12,228 --> 00:00:16,108
Johnston, in North Carolina,
in April. Taylor in May.

5
00:00:16,158 --> 00:00:19,122
Kirby Smith, in Texas, in June.

6
00:00:19,462 --> 00:00:24,682
June nineteenth: at Galveston, an order
tells Texas that all slaves are free.

7
00:00:24,732 --> 00:00:29,122
That's Juneteenth. Four days later,
Stand Watie, a Cherokee general,

8
00:00:29,172 --> 00:00:30,762
gives up his command.

9
00:00:31,102 --> 00:00:34,442
Then nothing, until November
sixth, in Liverpool,

10
00:00:34,492 --> 00:00:37,992
England. The raider Shenandoah had
been hunting whalers in the Pacific.

11
00:00:38,042 --> 00:00:40,942
Its captain learned the
war was over in August,

12
00:00:40,992 --> 00:00:43,538
and sailed for England to surrender.

13
00:00:43,858 --> 00:00:47,218
Two hundred and eleven
days after Appomattox.

14
00:00:47,268 --> 00:00:49,838
Every date with its source, on one line.

15
00:00:50,128 --> 00:00:52,537
That's OpenChronology, and it's free.
